WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
 - So hello, welcome to Python for Everybody.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.720
 I'm Charles Severance, your instructor.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.480
 And in this assignment, I mean in this video,

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.840
 I want to work through exercise 2.2.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.360
 And it's just a basic hello, kind of hello world.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.520
 And so I'm going to show you how I want you to do these things.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.480
 And I've got this folder called Python for Everybody.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.560
 And I'm going to use the Atom text editor.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.320
 So the whole thing is it's supposed to say, hello,

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.480
 enter your name.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.080
 There's the last one we did.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.360
 So I'm going to make in Python for Everybody.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.640
 I'm going to make a new folder.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.920
 New folder.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:48.200
 And I'm going to say ex02.02.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.320
 Make myself an exercise.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.960
 And I'm going to create a new file.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:01.720
 And I'm going to say print hello, and I'm going to say file,

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:02.920
 save as.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.800
 And it's important to get these in the right folder.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:12.400
 And so I'm going to call this ex02.py.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.600
 Always end these in Python because then you

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.760
 get the syntax highlighting.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.440
 OK, so now, again, you're not going to like this.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.360
 But I want you to learn to execute these things using

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.000
 the tool, the real tools that we're going to really use.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.080
 Because when you're applying this,

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.080
 I want you to know how to actually do real work.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.000
 So I need to get to the point where this terminal program--

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.320
 and I could say start run terminal.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.680
 That's another way to get the terminal program started.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.600
 But I've got her down here in my docs,

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.920
 so that saves me some time.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.640
 And of course, if you're on Windows,

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.600
 this is the command prompt.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.600
 And so in Windows, you'd type cd.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.200
 But in Mac, I take pwd.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.160
 And that shows where I am at in the directory structure.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.600
 And I can go into my desktop.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.560
 This would work the same.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.000
 And I can do an ls.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.480
 In Windows, I would say dir.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:14.800
 And I see this desktop folder.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.240
 And I'm going to go into py4e.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.000
 And if I keep doing pwd, you see I'm

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.680
 navigating deeper and deeper into this directory structure.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.800
 And then I do an ls here.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.880
 And I'm going to cd one more time to a folder.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.240
 Oh, I did that really quick.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:30.880
 I didn't tell you what I was doing.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.200
 cd ex tab.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.080
 So that's because if the name of the filer folder is unique,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.560
 you can hit the tab in the command line.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:40.480
 And away we go.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:47.120
 And so now we're in this folder, in the users folder,

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.520
 py4e, desktop, py4e, ex0202.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.320
 And then if I say ls, and that's what you want to get to.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.600
 You want to get to where you know

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.240
 where you're at in the folder structure.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.520
 And you know the file that you're going to run.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:00.640
 And I've saved this.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:07.400
 And so if I say python3 ex02.py, it runs.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:10.920
 And now I know that what I'm doing in this screen

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.440
 can be run in this screen.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.320
 And oh, I did that again.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.840
 I hit the up arrow, and I can run it again.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:20.200
 So you'll find that the way you write these programs is

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.960
 you write them here, and then write them here,

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:25.360
 and run them here.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.240
 And again, there are shortcuts.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.920
 And those are crutches, and you might like them.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.680
 But I just want you to learn how to use your computer

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.920
 like a real person uses the computer.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.280
 Because that's when we start dealing

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.120
 with databases and files, you're going

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.120
 to want to know where you're at on your computer.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.680
 And so if there's some fancy clicky button thing that

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.760
 says automatically run Python, that's convenient.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.380
 But I want you to know all this stuff.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.440
 OK, enough about how-- well, probably I'll just

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.320
 keep talking about that all the time.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:02.000
 So now that we've got our problem,

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
 write a program that uses input to prompt for their user name

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.480
 and welcomes them.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.640
 So this is pretty simple.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.000
 Go back to Adam.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.920
 Come on, Adam.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:21.600
 OK, so we have to have a variable NZT.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.400
 Well, later we'll talk about choosing variable names.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.320
 Right now I'm going to choose a crazy variable name.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.880
 Input is a function, and then you put a prompt.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:38.680
 And that's supposed to be enter your name, colon.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.960
 I don't want to put a space in there.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:45.680
 So I'm going to save that and just run it for yucks.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.080
 And there you go.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:51.880
 That's one thing you'll get used to is just

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:53.720
 type in gibberish really quick.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.040
 And then we're going to say print.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.560
 What do we got to say here?

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:58.280
 Print hello.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:10.600
 And go pull that variable back out.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:11.280
 Save that.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.960
 Command S is how I'm saying that.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.520
 Save command S. I saved it.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.080
 So I didn't make file save, but I could have said file save.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.120
 But command S saves it.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:25.440
 And now I can run it again.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:26.880
 Zap.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.640
 Hello, Zap.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:29.880
 Now I can run my thing.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:31.960
 Oh, and command K clears it.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:33.680
 I don't remember what it is on Windows,

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:36.440
 but there is a way to clear your screen.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:38.000
 I like to keep my screens clean.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.040
 Hello, Chuck.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:48.960
 And so we have completed exercise 2.2 with a little program.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.000
 Oh, and by the way, I'm using Alt Tab.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.160
 When you see this, I'm using the Alt Command Tab.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.040
 And the same thing works on Windows as Alt Tab.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.520
 OK?

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:02.920
 OK, so I hope that was helpful to you.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:05.760
 Just to kind of walk through one of the assignments,

